---
/**
 * Pricing and licence.
 *
 * The price itself is a placeholder. It is set on CodeCanyon and changes there, and
 * typing a number into a static page that nobody will remember to update is how a site
 * ends up advertising a price it does not charge. See docs/proposals/website.md.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/Placeholder.astro';
---

<Base
	title="Pricing and licence — Ultimate Client Manager"
	description="One payment, through CodeCanyon. No monthly fee, no per seat charge, and the source is included."
	current="/pricing/"
>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<p class="kicker">Pricing</p>
			<h1>One payment. No subscription.</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Ultimate Client Manager is sold through CodeCanyon, which handles the payment and
				the licence. You pay once and the software is yours to install.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a class="button" href="https://codecanyon.net/item/freelance-project-invoice-crm/2621629">
					See the current price on CodeCanyon
				</a>
			</div>
			<p class="under">
				Current price: <Placeholder what="price, and whether a Regular and an Extended licence are both offered" />
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="tint">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>What the payment includes</h2>
			<ul class="grid">
				<li class="card">
					<h3>The whole application</h3>
					<p>
						Every feature. There is no edition with the good parts removed, and nothing is
						unlocked by a second purchase.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="card">
					<h3>Unlimited staff accounts</h3>
					<p>There is no per seat charge. Add as many people as the business has.</p>
				</li>
				<li class="card">
					<h3>The source code</h3>
					<p>
						It is PHP, and you get all of it. Change it, read it, or have somebody else
						work on it.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="card">
					<h3>Updates</h3>
					<p>
						The application checks for updates and installs them when you tell it to.
						<Placeholder what="whether updates are included indefinitely, as Envato's licence does not itself say so" />
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="card">
					<h3>Support</h3>
					<p>
						Through the item's comments page on CodeCanyon.
						<a href="/support/">What that covers</a>.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li class="card">
					<h3>Documentation</h3>
					<p>
						<a href="/docs/">All of it</a>, public and free to read before you buy, plus a
						copy inside the download that works with no internet connection.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section>
		<div class="wrap narrow">
			<h2>What it does not include</h2>
			<p>
				<strong>Hosting.</strong> You install it on an account you already have or one you
				buy separately. <a href="/requirements/">What it needs</a> is modest and most
				shared hosting meets it.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Payment processing fees.</strong> Stripe, PayPal and Square charge their
				own fees on money you take. Nothing extra is added on top by this software — there
				is no transaction cut.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Custom development.</strong> If you want something built, that is a
				separate arrangement.
			</p>

			<h2>The licence</h2>
			<p>
				Your purchase grants an Envato Market licence — Regular or Extended, depending on
				which you buy — and the terms are published at
				<a href="https://codecanyon.net/licenses/standard">codecanyon.net/licenses/standard</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				That licence covers the application code and the design made for it. The third
				party components bundled inside keep their own licences, and every one of them is
				listed with its licence text in the <code>Licensing</code> folder of the download.
				<a href="/docs/reference/credits/">Credits and licences</a> explains how the two fit
				together, including the handful of components whose licence file was missing and
				what was established about each.
			</p>

			<div class="note">
				<p>
					<strong>There is no licence enforcement in the software.</strong> It does not
					check a key, phone home, or stop working. The honesty of paying for it is left
					to you, which is how it has always been.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
